\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces \emph {thelab-mcp} architecture: three ways in (CLI, HTTP/UI, MCP) over one deterministic core; the agent layer explores, the approval gate decides, the factory executes, and the dashed feedback returns replay-verified champion configurations to the baseline (the ratchet loop). All state lands in local, inspectable stores.}}{12}{figure.caption.9}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.2}{\ignorespaces Experiment lifecycle state machine (persisted per experiment).}}{23}{figure.caption.13}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.3}{\ignorespaces Workspace UI: the deterministic Data view, with dataset preview, EDA modules (missing values, distributions, correlations) and the cleaning report.}}{24}{figure.caption.14}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.4}{\ignorespaces Workspace UI: the agentic Experiments view, with recorded stage pipeline (Plan, Clean, Train, Evaluate, Agent round), job timeline, best-run card, and the analyst findings brief.}}{25}{figure.caption.15}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.1}{\ignorespaces Runs per active day, generated from run-manifest timestamps. The early peaks correspond to the B1 benchmark sweep (Aug 24) and real-dataset hardening (Aug 29); the Sep 3--5 surge is the live agentic-round recording campaign and the multi-domain ratchet loop.}}{44}{figure.caption.22}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.2}{\ignorespaces Run count by model family. Random forest and logistic regression dominate by count; every registered family appears in recorded use.}}{44}{figure.caption.23}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.3}{\ignorespaces Test-accuracy distribution of completed classification runs. The distribution now spreads across the 0.6--0.9 range on real datasets, with the fixture-driven mass at $\geq 0.99$ a visible minority; the honest left tail (0.33--0.67) remains recorded evidence that determinism guarantees repeatability, not quality.}}{44}{figure.caption.24}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.4}{\ignorespaces Batch entries vs valid entries across the validator generations: every pre-fix failure was caught by the deterministic validator, and every post-fix round completed at validity 1.0.}}{49}{figure.caption.30}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.5}{\ignorespaces Per-dataset recorded agentic-round wall-clock (sum of round seconds across all generations; the S\&P 500 dataset dominates at 1{,}995\,s).}}{53}{figure.caption.35}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.6}{\ignorespaces Ratchet uplift per dataset: deterministic try-all baseline (grey) vs absorbed champion (blue); deltas annotated.}}{53}{figure.caption.36}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.7}{\ignorespaces Arm B: best value per driver team and dataset (accuracy for kidney/clinvar/crypto, $R^2$ for energy). No team dominates: commercial and open teams each won two datasets, llama-uniform the other two.}}{57}{figure.caption.42}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.8}{\ignorespaces Prediction latency per surface over the 17 absorbed champions (CLI, HTTP, MCP; milliseconds). Inference is deterministic and token-free on every surface.}}{58}{figure.caption.43}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.9}{\ignorespaces The grounded chat agent enforcing the metric-evidence gate live: the agent cited \texttt {test\_accuracy}=1.0 for a run whose recorded evidence is 0.8045, and the grounding layer refused the answer instead of showing it (red).}}{60}{figure.caption.45}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.10}{\ignorespaces Feature-engineering transform cost per driver: completion tokens per round with provider-reported cost (deepseek-v4-flash reasoning burn vs GLM open route).}}{62}{figure.caption.48}%
